Then came eighty-one, on the dark winter track,  
When the ghosts of the past tried to batter their way back.  
The twenty-third day of the February rain,  
When a madness returned to the heart of old Spain.  
Tejero marched into the Congress of State,  
With a pistol held high and a cargo of hate.

He fired at the ceiling, he ordered the floor,  
“Stay quiet, you bastards, or look at the door!”  
The tanks rolled in Valencia, Milans del Bosch arose,  
To strangle the freedom that everyone chose.  
The army was waiting for word from the throne,  
To turn the young kingdom back into a stone.

The king did not falter, he stood in the light,  
And locked himself deep in his office that night.  
He picked up the phone, and with fierce, royal pride,  
He ordered the generals back to their side.

“The Crown will not stand for a treasonous blow!  
Return to your barracks! The answer is No!”

× × ×

At one in the morning, the television screen  
Showed the king in his uniform, tall and serene.  
He spoke to the nation, he spoke to the pack,  
And drove the dark shadows of history back.  
“The Constitution lives! It will never comply  
With those who would make the young liberty die!”

The coup was deflated, the soldiers withdrew,  
And Tejero’s rebels surrendered their crew.  
The communist leaders, the left and the right,  
All cheered for the Bourbon who won the great fight.  
He had saved the young freedom of Spain with his word,  
A champion of law who had silenced the sword.

× × ×

The years spun their glory, then crumbled to rust,  
As the gold of the monarch was turned into dust.  
Exiled in the desert, away from his land,  
The King of the Crisis sits lone in the sand.

Though the history books  
carry his scandals and pain,  
They remember the night  
    that he saved  
        the whole of Spain.
